            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "gl_color sets the plain colour things are drawn in when lighting is off.\n\nLIGHTING IGNORES IT:\ngl_context switches lighting on, and while it is on, OpenGL takes a shape's colour from the material and the lights - the current colour is not used at all. So gl_color placed before a lit sphere changes nothing. To see it, switch lighting off for that branch with gl_enable GL_LIGHTING, unticked.\n\nWith lighting off a shape is drawn in exactly this colour, an even patch with no shading and no sense of form. That is right for markers, lines and anything schematic; gl_material and a light are for anything meant to look like an object.\n\ngl_text, gl_line_array and gl_button_grid set colours of their own and are not affected.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nThe colour applies from where it sits onward and the previous colour is restored at the end of its branch.\n\nIT STARTS INVISIBLE:\nAll four sliders start at 0 - black, and fully transparent. Blending is on in the context, so until 'alpha' is raised, what it colours cannot be seen.\n\nTHIS IS THE OLDER SYSTEM:\nmgl_ nodes do the same job in the newer scene graph, with real materials,  shaders and a main-thread guard on the drawing. For anything new, use those.\n\nThese remain because patches are built on them, because gl_body and its  relatives live in this world, and because the fixed-function pipeline they use  is sometimes simpler to reason about when all you want is a few shapes.\n\nSYNTAX:\ngl_color\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\ngl_color\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ngl chain in:\nThe chain. This triggers the node.\n\nred / green / blue / alpha:\nThe colour, each 0 to 1. All 0 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngl chain out:\nThe chain, with this colour in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\ngl_enable to switch lighting off; gl_material for lit surfaces. mgl_color is the newer equivalent, and does tint lit shapes.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 880,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "gl_color sets the plain colour things are drawn in when lighting is off.\n\nLIGHTING IGNORES IT:\ngl_context switches lighting on, and while it is on, OpenGL takes a shape's colour from the material and the lights - the current colour is not used at all. So gl_color placed before a lit sphere changes nothing. To see it, switch lighting off for that branch with gl_enable GL_LIGHTING, unticked.\n\nWith lighting off a shape is drawn in exactly this colour, an even patch with no shading and no sense of form. That is right for markers, lines and anything schematic; gl_material and a light are for anything meant to look like an object.\n\ngl_text, gl_line_array and gl_button_grid set colours of their own and are not affected.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nThe colour applies from where it sits onward and the previous colour is restored at the end of its branch.\n\nIT STARTS INVISIBLE:\nAll four sliders start at 0 - black, and fully transparent. Blending is on in the context, so until 'alpha' is raised, what it colours cannot be seen.\n\nTHIS IS THE OLDER SYSTEM:\nmgl_ nodes do the same job in the newer scene graph, with real materials,  shaders and a main-thread guard on the drawing. For anything new, use those.\n\nThese remain because patches are built on them, because gl_body and its  relatives live in this world, and because the fixed-function pipeline they use  is sometimes simpler to reason about when all you want is a few shapes.\n\nSYNTAX:\ngl_color\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\ngl_color\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ngl chain in:\nThe chain. This triggers the node.\n\nred / green / blue / alpha:\nThe colour, each 0 to 1. All 0 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngl chain out:\nThe chain, with this colour in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\ngl_enable to switch lighting off; gl_material for lit surfaces. mgl_color is the newer equivalent, and does tint lit shapes."
        },
